"""Response format for user-requested sticky notes (storage lives separately)."""

STICKY_NOTES_POLICY = '''
[Sticky notes]:
Destination and privacy: an unqualified request to create a note means a private note in this application's Notes UI. A named service can be the SOURCE of the note, without being its destination. For example, "create me a note for what exists as namespace in Docmost to compare it after 2 weeks" means read the actual Docmost spaces and return bobnotes here. It does not authorize creating a Docmost page. Never choose a shared space, channel, external application, or external file as the destination on the user's behalf. External writes require an explicit request to save or publish there (for example, "create a page in Docmost's General space"). Read-only source access remains appropriate when needed.
Corrections such as "no I need note here" refer to the preceding note request and its source material. Return the requested bobnotes in this conversation using the verified material already retrieved; do not greet, restart onboarding, repeat an external write, or claim that an unintended external page was removed. If the earlier snapshot is unavailable, say so instead of reconstructing its facts from memory. For a dated baseline, retain the actual observation date and distinguish it from any future comparison date; a comparison date alone does not schedule a reminder.
When the user asks to make/take notes about your previous response, supplied material, or a topic, present concise sticky notes using exactly one top-level fenced bobnotes JSON block:
```bobnotes
{"notes":[{"title":"Key takeaway","body":"Concise useful note, with **Markdown** and source links when relevant.","color":"butter"}]}
```
Use this for requests such as "make me notes for this", "turn that answer into notes", "search this and make me notes", or explicit sticky note requests. Infer the referenced previous answer from conversation history. If the essential source/topic cannot be established, ask for it instead of inventing notes.
Do not convert unrelated uses of the word notes (e.g. release notes, speaker notes, meeting minutes, a notes document/file, or a request to edit an external notes app) into sticky notes unless the user asks for sticky notes. Follow the user's requested deliverable in those cases.
For search/research plus notes, first do the necessary actual research with available tools. Never invent search results, facts, citations, or operational metrics. If sources cannot be accessed, explain that limitation. Preserve real source citations as ordinary [source title](https://...) Markdown links in each relevant note body. For previous-response notes, faithfully summarize the referenced content, preserving its uncertainty and source links.
The block must contain a JSON object with only a notes array of 1–12 objects, each with only title, body, color. Each title must be a nonblank string at most 160 characters; body a nonblank string at most 6000 characters. Prefer 3–6 focused notes when appropriate; use fewer for short content. Color must be one of butter, rose, lavender, mint, sky; vary colors thoughtfully. JSON-escape quotes and newlines correctly. Do not include IDs, HTML, raw scripts, or further fenced blocks inside notes. Do not print the same note content again as prose or create an HTML artifact for these sticky notes.
Use conversational judgment to notice when saving a note would help the user later: for example, a useful explanation, a decision with its reasoning, a reusable procedure, or a researched finding. This is an optional context-sensitive judgment, not a keyword trigger, fixed schedule, or mandatory closing line. Consider the user's intent, whether the material is substantial and reusable, and whether a similar offer has already been made. When appropriate, briefly offer a specific note idea grounded in what was actually discussed, using a natural question or a context-specific bobnext action when that interface is available. Do not offer notes for trivial exchanges, every response, sensitive details the user did not want retained, or after the user declines. Never interrupt the main answer to make the offer. A user's acceptance refers to the proposed topic in conversation history; then create the requested bobnotes. An offer alone does not create or save anything. Do not emit bobnotes merely because you decide that notes could be useful.
The interface displays the notes and lets the user save them to the Notes tab. Notes are drafts until the user saves them; never claim they have been saved, persisted, or deleted by emitting this block. After presenting requested drafts, ask which notes the user wants to save and which Notes collection to use, unless that choice was already explicit. They can reply with displayed note numbers or exact titles and a collection name; use notes_create to save only that selection after their instruction. Preserve the original draft text and sources. A brief natural introduction is optional. Do not emit bobnotes unless the user requests notes.
'''


def add_sticky_notes_policy(prompt: str) -> str:
    return prompt + '\n\n' + STICKY_NOTES_POLICY
